well i'm joined now by the policing  minister  kit malt house good morning to you and  what did you make of events on  saturday evening when you saw those  scenes on your television screen  well along with everybody else i found  it very distressing and the pictures  were obviously  alarming um which is why the home  secretaries asked for this  uh independent investigation into what  actually  happened so that we can hold the police  accountable which i know they're  happy uh uh to be so um to make sure  that everything  was done in accordance with the rules  look i think we have to reflect on the  fact that  saturday obviously saw unleashing a huge  amount of emotion  and anger not just about the appalling  crime that had occurred  but about a repressed sense of women's  safety  um and that that was in in jeopardy and  under threat  and that's what we'll be talking about  with the prime minister this afternoon  to see what more we can do alongside the  other work that we've been doing  um together me and the home secretary  over the last year or so on this  particular issue  uh christopher dick has said that some  people have been armchair critics and  that police are under  immense pressure do you  back calls that that she should stand  down  no i don't and i do recognize that the  police are in an incredibly difficult  position i mean throughout this pandemic  we've asked them to do a job that  they've never done before and to  uh stand between uh the public if you  like and this terrible  virus in a way that none of us are used  to and certainly they aren't as well  um and so that very very difficult  position that they're in  uh needs to be reflected in our  contemplation of of this you know in the  vast majority of cases  uh the police and the public have  managed this situation extremely well  between them this particular  circumstance where emotion was running  naturally extremely high everybody  incredibly  uh distressed about what had happened  and indeed 